nter-channel phase loss. Tested on first-order ambisonics environment transfer and seismic cross-station translation—two physically unrelated domains—RIPPLE outperforms recovery-based pipelines on the coherence metrics that downstream analyses consume. The seismic case is decisive: across architecturally distinct generators, per-channel recovery leaves S-wave polarization error near the 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} random expectation, whereas learned phase reduces it to 33.8 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ} . Introduct\n...\n</persisted-output>", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "adb0bb30-86c7-403b-bcca-205f6e39bd4b", "uuid": "ef7873dc-d92b-4feb-b22f-2789b1722956", "timestamp": "2026-07-31T05:33:08.887Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": " RIPPLE: Generating Multi-Channel Phase, Not Recovering It Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2607.27775v1 [cs.LG] 30 Jul 2026 RIPPLE: Generating Multi-Channel Phase, Not Recovering It Jaehyuk Lee 1 , Yeajin Lee 2 , Dayeon Shin 1 , Donghun Lee 1 \\corresponding Abstract Generative models synthesize magnitude spectra with high fidelity, while phase is delegated to a recovery module—Griffin–Lim, a vocoder, or a latent decoder—applied independently to each channel. For multi-channel waveforms this delegation is costly: the physical content of spatial audio and three-component seismograms lives in the phase relationships between channels, precisely what channel-independent recovery cannot produce. The cost is also invisible, since the magnitude-based metrics common to both fields barely move when inter-channel phase coherence collapses—so a pipeline can discard the physical information in its output while still scoring well. We argue that phase should be generated, not recovered, and present RIPPLE (Rectified Inter-channel Phase with Prior-based LEarning), which reinterprets Griffin–Lim as a phase prior rather than a final estimator: initialized from the source phase, this prior carries the inter-channel structure to be preserved, and a rectified flow refines it toward the target under an explicit inter-channel phase loss. Tested on first-order ambisonics environment transfer and seismic cross-station translation—two physically unrelated domains—RIPPLE outperforms recovery-based pipelines on the coherence metrics that downstream analyses consume. The seismic case is decisive: across architecturally distinct generators, per-channel recovery leaves S-wave polarization error near the 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} random expectation, whereas learned phase reduces it to 33.8 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ} . Introduction Generative modeling of waveforms has advanced largely by splitting the short-time Fourier transform (STFT) into a magnitude that is generated and a phase that is reconstructed . This division has been productive for single-channel signals, where a magnitude-consistent phase estimate usually suffices, so phase has been handed off to separate recovery or decoding modules. The assumption no longer holds for multi-channel waveforms, for which the inter-channel relationship itself carries the information of interest. In first-order ambisonics (FOA), source azimuth and elevation are encoded in the relative phase and amplitude of the W/Y/Z/X channels; in three-component (Z/N/E) seismograms, P- and S-wave arrival times and particle motion are jointly determined by the relative amplitudes and the coherent phase structure across components. Independent per-channel phase recovery does not merely degrade inter-channel information; it can eliminate it altogether. This limitation is not specific to any one recovery mechanism. We measure the routes both fields use under oracle conditions: Griffin–Lim on ground-truth magnitudes, a strong neural vocoder (BigVGAN), a spatial codec round-trip, and a seismic latent-VAE round-trip. All of them fail in the same way. In spatial audio, azimuth error reaches the chance level ( π / 2 \\pi/2 ); in seismic data, S-wave polarization error increases from 3.7 ∘ → 42.9 ∘ 3.7^{\\circ}\\to 42.9^{\\circ} , whereas amplitude-based GOF remains high. These errors arise with no generative model in the loop (Table 5 ). Post-hoc phase recovery imposes a ceiling that no generator built on it can exceed. Two facts let this failure ship unnoticed. The consumer of these waveforms is not a listener but an algorithm—a localizer reading direction from a re-rendered intensity vector, or a polarization analysis reading particle motion—so it consumes exactly the inter-channel phase that per-channel recovery destroys; and that destruction is silent, since the waveform-, spectral-, and magnitude-based goodness-of-fit metrics both fields default to barely move when inter-channel phase lands at or near chance. Learning phase directly is awkward: it wraps around on 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} and is entangled multiplicatively with magnitude. The common response is to avoid learning it at all. We take the opposite position: for multi-channel waveforms, phase is not a nuisance variable to be recovered but a structured target to be generated, and the structure classical estimators capture should enter as a prior, not as the final answer. What existing approaches miss. According to our survey, summarized in Table 1 , no prior work pairs learned phase with source coherence, a cross-channel objective, and coherence-sensitive evaluation. In other words, phase is handled without an inter-channel objective, kept outside the generative model—delegated to per-channel recovery or a compressed latent—or generated per channel with no coherence target. Such approaches cannot extend well to FOA; the authors of Diff-SAGe make the same argument, and tries to remedy it by modeling phase explicitly as a complex spectrogram (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) . We take that argument one step further and make inter-channel coherence itself the training target. At the same time, we observe that representing phase explicitly does not by itself produce phase that is coherent across channels. When we adapt Diff-SAGe to our source-conditioned setting, its phase fidelity degrades rather than transfers (Table 2 ). Seismology fits the same pattern, with phase recovered by per-channel Griffin–Lim (Jung et al. 2025 ) or test-time retrieval (Bi et al. 2025 ) . Why translation, and why a prior. When two waveforms from the same source can be paired, such that the same underlying signal is realized through two transfer functions (e.g. two room impulse responses, two geological paths), phase generation can be reframed as translation from a source to a target. Unlike synthesis from mono or semantic input, which must conjure coherence from a signal that has none, translation need only transport it. We use Griffin–Lim as phase prior to seed this structure, turning phase generation into a correction problem (quantified in our ablations). Contributions. We present RIPPLE ( R ectified I nter-channel P hase with P rior-based LE arning) contributing: • Utilizing Griffin–Lim as a phase prior, not a phase decoder. Initialized from the source phase, it supplies magnitude consistency without destroying source coherence, reducing phase generation to a residual correction. • Decoupled two-stage rectified flow with an inter-channel phase-difference (IPD) objective. Independent timesteps ( t m , t p ) (t_{m},t_{p}) decorrelate the two modalities’ learning signals, and the inter-channel phase-difference loss makes coherence an explicit training target. • Cross-domain empirical validation. On FOA environment transfer and on seismic cross-station translation—a physically unrelated domain—RIPPLE outperforms recovery- and vocoder-based pipelines on coherence metrics, most sharply on S-wave polarization. Related Work Multi-channel spatial audio generation. Neural multi-channel synthesis spans mono-to-binaural rendering (Richard et al. 2021 ; Leng et al. 2022 ; Lee and Lee 2023 ; Liang et al. 2025 ) , FOA generation via spatial codecs (Heydari et al. 2025 ) or complex-spectrogram flow transformers (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) , and video- or language-conditioned models (Liu et al. 2025 ; Templin et al. 2025 ) , all mapping mono or semantic input, which carries no inter-channel phase structure, to spatial output. Seismic waveform and ground-motion generation. Three-component waveforms have been synthesized with conditional GANs (Wang et al. 2021 ; Li et al. 2024 ) and latent diffusion on spectrograms (Bergmeister et al. 2025 ; Bi et al. 2025 ) . In all of these, phase is recovered rather than learned—via test-time phase retrieval (Bi et al. 2025 ) or per-channel Griffin–Lim as in HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) ; we adopt the latter as our directly comparable baseline to isolate whether learning multi-component phase improves inter-component coherence. Table 1: Positioning of the closest surveyed work. Rec : phase delegated to recovery; impl. : waveform generation without separate phase; Gen : phase as explicit target. “–”: not established by the cited source; △ \\triangle : partial. Full version with representations and I/O settings in Appendix A. Method Phase Src. coh. X-loss Coh. eval Mono/video/lang. synth. 1 impl. × \\times × \\times – BinauralFlow 2 Gen × \\times × \\times △ \\triangle Tango+voc. / ImmerseDiff. 3 Rec × \\times × \\times × \\times Diff-SAGe 4 Gen × \\times × \\times △ \\triangle FOA Tokenizer 5 Rec n/a △ \\triangle – GANs / latent diffusion 6 impl./Rec × \\times × \\times – HEGGS 7 Rec ✓ × \\times × \\times RIPPLE (ours) Gen ✓ ✓ ✓ 1 Richard et al. ( 2021 ); Leng et al. ( 2022 ); Lee and Lee ( 2023 ); Liu et al. ( 2025 ); Templin et al. ( 2025 ) ; 2 Liang et al. ( 2025 ) ; 3 Ghosal et al. ( 2023 ); Lee et al. ( 2023 ); Heydari et al. ( 2025 ) ; 4 Kushwaha et al. ( 2025 ) ; 5 Sudarsanam et al. ( 2025 ) ; 6 Wang et al. ( 2021 ); Li et al. ( 2024 ); Bergmeister et al. ( 2025 ); Bi et al. ( 2025 ) ; 7 Jung et al. ( 2025 ) . Phase recovery and neural vocoders. The Griffin–Lim algorithm (Griffin and Lim 1984 ) estimates a magnitude-consistent phase by alternating projections. Neural vocoders replace this with learned synthesis from magnitude-like features—adversarially trained waveform generators such as HiFi-GAN and BigVGAN (Kong et al. 2020 ; Lee et al. 2023 ) , or Fourier-coefficient prediction as in Vocos (Siuzdak 2024 ) —but operate per signal, without a cross-channel objective; FOA Tokenizer (Sudarsanam et al. 2025 ) adds a spatial-consistency loss, though for compression rather than generation. Flow matching and rectified flows for audio. Flow matching (Lipman et al. 2023 ) , stochastic interpolants (Albergo and Vanden-Eijnden 2023 ) , and rectified flow (Liu et al. 2023b ) are now widely used for audio (Guan et al. 2024 ; Liu et al. 2025 ; Liang et al. 2025 ) ; manifold-native variants instead define the interpolant on the geometry itself (Mathieu and Nickel 2020 ; Chen and Lipman 2024 ) , an alternative we discuss in Appendix D.2 . Diffusion-bridge methods (Liu et al. 2023a ; Zhou et al. 2024 ) likewise start from an informed endpoint rather than noise, and multi-modal diffusion has used per-modality timesteps (Bao et al. 2023 ) . Our contribution is not the transport machinery but where transport starts from: a magnitude-consistent, source-coherent Griffin–Lim prior. Positioning. Table 1 makes the comparison explicit: within the surveyed work, RIPPLE is the only method that pairs learned phase with source coherence, a cross-channel objective, and coherence-sensitive evaluation (full version in Appendix A ). Method Figure 1: Overview of RIPPLE. Stage 1 predicts the target magnitude; the source-informed Griffin–Lim prior is built from it; Stage 2 integrates the phase flow from this prior. Problem Setup We study source-to-target translation of multi-channel waveforms. Given a C C -channel source w ( s ) ∈ ℝ C × L w^{(\\mathrm{s})}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L} and a target w ( t ) ∈ ℝ C × L w^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times L} that is the same underlying source signal passed through a different transfer function, together with a target conditioning vector c ∈ ℝ d c c\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}} (position / room / distance metadata), we learn the conditional distribution p ​ ( w ( t ) ∣ w ( s ) , c ) p(w^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\mid w^{(\\mathrm{s})},c) . Our two instantiations are FOA spatial audio ( C = 4 C{=}4 ) and three-component seismograms ( C = 3 C{=}3 ); see Experimental Setup in Appendix B . Spectrogram Representation Let X = ℱ ​ { w } ∈ ℂ C × F × T X=\\mathcal{F}\\{w\\}\\in\\mathbb{C}^{C\\times F\\times T} be the complex STFT. We represent it in the disentangled form Z = ( log ⁡ | X | , cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ) ∈ ℝ 3 ​ C × F × T . Z=\\big(\\log|X|,\\ \\cos\\angle X,\\ \\sin\\angle X\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{3C\\times F\\times T}. (1) Two design choices motivate this. (a) 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} phase embedding. Phase ϕ ∈ 𝕊 1 \\phi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{1} is embedded into ℝ 2 \\mathbb{R}^{2} as ( cos ⁡ ϕ , sin ⁡ ϕ ) (\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\phi) , removing the wrap-around discontinuity by construction: naive regression penalizes the identical points ϕ = ± π \\phi{=}\\pm\\pi by ( 2 ​ π ) 2 ≈ 39 (2\\pi)^{2}{\\approx}39 . (b) Magnitude/phase disentanglement. Keeping magnitude and phase as separate fields, rather than folding them into ( ℜ ⁡ X , ℑ ⁡ X ) (\\Re X,\\Im X) , lets magnitude serve as conditioning information for phase—the property our Griffin–Lim prior and magnitude-weighted phase loss both exploit—and lets the two modalities carry different priors. Griffin–Lim as a Magnitude-Consistent Phase Prior The Griffin–Lim algorithm GL K ​ ( m ; ϕ init ) \\mathrm{GL}_{K}(m;\\phi_{\\text{init}}) seeks, by alternating projections, a phase ϕ \\phi such that m ⊙ e i ​ ϕ m\\odot e^{i\\phi} is a consistent STFT with magnitude m (i.e., a fixed point of ℱ ​ { ℱ − 1 ​ { ⋅ } } \\mathcal{F}\\{\\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\{\\cdot\\}\\} ), iterating for k = 0 , … , K − 1 k=0,\\dots,K{-}1 : ϕ ( k + 1 ) = ∠ ​ ℱ ​ { ℱ − 1 ​ { m ⊙ e i ​ ϕ ( k ) } } , ϕ ( 0 ) = ϕ init . \\phi^{(k+1)}=\\angle\\,\\mathcal{F}\\!\\big\\{\\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\{m\\odot e^{i\\phi^{(k)}}\\}\\big\\},\\qquad\\phi^{(0)}=\\phi_{\\text{init}}. (2) Standard usage treats ϕ ( K ) \\phi^{(K)} as the final phase estimate. We instead treat it as an informed prior for flow matching. The iteration is applied independently per channel, so Griffin–Lim itself cannot generate inter-channel phase relationships; whatever coherence the prior carries must come from its initialization ϕ init \\phi_{\\text{init}} . Source-informed initialization. We initialize from the source phase, ϕ init = ∠ ​ X ( s ) \\phi_{\\text{init}}=\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})} . Because the target and source share an underlying source signal, the source phase carries inter-channel structure that is correlated with the target’s, though the two transfer functions generally reshape it per frequency. Per-channel Griffin–Lim iterations gradually attenuate this structure. We add a small Gaussian perturbation to p 0 p_{0} , giving the perturbed prior of Eq. 3 : p 0 \\displaystyle p_{0} = ( cos ⁡ ϕ GL , sin ⁡ ϕ GL ) + σ prior ​ ϵ , \\displaystyle=\\big(\\cos\\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}},\\,\\sin\\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}}\\big)+\\sigma_{\\text{prior}}\\,\\epsilon, (3) ϕ GL \\displaystyle\\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}} = GL K ​ ( m ( t ) ; ∠ ​ X ( s ) ) , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) . \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{GL}_{K}\\!\\big(m^{(\\mathrm{t})};\\,\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})}\\big),\\quad\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I). The perturbation trains the velocity field on a neighborhood of the prior rather than the exact prior alone—inference builds p 0 p_{0} from the predicted m ^ \\hat{m} , not the oracle m ( t ) m^{(t)} used in training—and takes p 0 p_{0} off the unit circle, supplying the radial support that integration in ℝ 2 \\mathbb{R}^{2} requires (App. D.2 ). Decoupled Two-Stage Rectified Flow We model magnitude and phase with two rectified flows that use independent interpolation timesteps (Fig. 1 ). Magnitude flow (source → \\rightarrow target). z t m mag \\displaystyle z^{\\text{mag}}_{t_{m}} = ( 1 − t m ) ​ log ⁡ | X ( s ) | + t m ​ log ⁡ | X ( t ) | , \\displaystyle=(1-t_{m})\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}|+t_{m}\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|, (4) v mag ∗ \\displaystyle v^{*}_{\\text{mag}} = log ⁡ | X ( t ) | − log ⁡ | X ( s ) | . \\displaystyle=\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|-\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}|. (5) Phase flow (Griffin–Lim prior → \\rightarrow target). z t p phase \\displaystyle z^{\\text{phase}}_{t_{p}} = ( 1 − t p ) ​ p 0 + t p ​ ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) , \\displaystyle=(1-t_{p})\\,p_{0}+t_{p}\\big(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\big), (6) v phase ∗ \\displaystyle v^{*}_{\\text{phase}} = ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) − p 0 . \\displaystyle=\\big(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\big)-p_{0}. The timesteps are sampled independently, t m , t p ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0 , 1 ) t_{m},t_{p}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0,1) . We use two separate velocity networks v θ mag v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta} and v θ phase v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta} that share the same conditioning encoder E c ​ ( c ) E_{c}(c) . Since magnitude is informative for phase, the input to v θ phase v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta} additionally concatenates log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| along the channel dimension, making the target magnitude available to the phase head throughout integration. Why decoupled timesteps. Since the two velocity networks share E c E_{c} , tying t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} correlates their training noise levels; independent sampling decorrelates them, and the effect concentrates on inter-channel coherence (Table 4 ). Why a Griffin–Lim prior helps. Starting from p 0 p_{0} rather than noise places the phase flow near the STFT-consistency manifold at the target magnitude, so it learns only a residual correction—shortening transport and letting short schedules suffice (Figure 4 ). Training Objective The overall training objective is a weighted sum of two velocity-regression losses, a magnitude-reconstruction loss, phase-quality terms, and an inter-channel phase-difference (IPD) loss. All phase-related losses are magnitude-weighted so that low-energy bins, where phase is informationally degenerate, do not dominate training. For convenience, we define the one-step prediction of each modality ∙ ∈ { mag , phase } \\bullet\\in\\{\\mathrm{mag},\\mathrm{phase}\\} as z ^ 1 | t ∙ = z t ∙ + ( 1 − t ) ​ v θ ∙ ​ ( ⋅ ) , \\hat{z}^{\\bullet}_{1|t}=z^{\\bullet}_{t}+(1-t)\\,v^{\\bullet}_{\\theta}(\\cdot), (7) Magnitude-aware weighting. We weight phase losses by a per-sample normalized sigmoid gate w ​ ( | X ( t ) | ) = σ ​ ( log ⁡ | X ( t ) | − μ s ) , w(|X^{(t)}|)=\\sigma\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log|X^{(t)}|-\\mu}{s}\\right), (8) where μ \\mu and s s are the per-channel mean and standard deviation of log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(t)}| over the ( f , t ) (f,t) axes of each sample, and w w is subsequently normalized to unit mean per channel; low-magnitude bins gracefully fall back to the prior. Velocity losses. For the two modalities, ℒ mag \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mag}} = 𝔼 ​ [ ‖ v θ mag − v mag ∗ ‖ 2 ] , \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\|v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta}-v^{*}_{\\text{mag}}\\|^{2}\\right], (9) ℒ phase vel \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{vel}}_{\\text{phase}} = 𝔼 ​ [ w ​ ( | X ( t ) | ) ​ ‖ v θ phase − v phase ∗ ‖ 2 ] . \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[w(|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|)\\,\\|v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta}-v^{*}_{\\text{phase}}\\|^{2}\\right]. (10) Inter-channel phase consistency (IPD) loss. The losses above are all channel-wise, so inter-channel coherence would only emerge as a byproduct. To make it an explicit training signal, we project predictions onto 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} by unit normalization, compute relative phase vectors r i ​ j = e i ​ ( ϕ i − ϕ j ) r_{ij}=e^{i(\\phi_{i}-\\phi_{j})} for each channel pair ( i , j ) (i,j) from both the prediction and the target, and maximize their inner product: ℒ IPD = 1 | 𝒫 | ​ ∑ ( i , j ) ∈ 𝒫 𝔼 ​ [ w i ​ j ​ ( 1 − ⟨ r ^ i ​ j , r i ​ j ∗ ⟩ ) ] , \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IPD}}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{P}|}\\!\\!\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{P}}\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[w_{ij}\\big(1-\\langle\\hat{r}_{ij},\\,r^{*}_{ij}\\rangle\\big)\\right], (11) where 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} is the set of channel pairs, ⟨ ⋅ , ⋅ ⟩ \\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle is the inner product of the two-dimensional unit vectors ( = cos ⁡ ( Δ ​ IPD ) =\\cos(\\Delta\\text{IPD}) ), and w i ​ j = σ ​ ( ( min ⁡ ( log ⁡ m i , log ⁡ m j ) − μ ) / s ) w_{ij}=\\sigma\\!\\bigl((\\min(\\log m_{i},\\log m_{j})-\\mu)/s\\bigr) applies the gating of (Eq. 8 ) with the element-wise minimum of the two channels’ log-magnitudes in place of log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(t)}| , so a bin contributes only where both channels are energetic. This loss is invariant to a common rotation of ϕ i \\phi_{i} and ϕ j \\phi_{j} (global phase shift invariance), matching the invariance of the physical quantities we evaluate (DOA and polarization direction) and thereby placing the learning signal directly on inter-channel structure. Auxiliary losses. Velocity regression alone enforces neither the unit-circle constraint nor endpoint consistency. From the one-step phase prediction z ^ 1 | t p phase = ( c ^ , s ^ ) \\hat{z}^{\\text{phase}}_{1|t_{p}}=(\\hat{c},\\hat{s}) we add ℒ phase q \\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}} , a weighted sum of a phase direction loss, an instantaneous-frequency loss on ∂ t ϕ \\partial_{t}\\phi , and a unit-norm penalty ( c ^ 2 + s ^ 2 − 1 ) 2 (\\hat{c}^{2}+\\hat{s}^{2}-1)^{2} ; and ℒ rec = 𝔼 ​ [ ‖ z ^ 1 | t m mag − log ⁡ | X ( t ) | ‖ 2 ] \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rec}}=\\mathbb{E}[\\|\\hat{z}^{\\text{mag}}_{1|t_{m}}-\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|\\|^{2}] , which reweights the magnitude velocity error by ( 1 − t m ) 2 (1-t_{m})^{2} to emphasize early timesteps. Definitions in App. B.3 . Total loss. The overall objective is ℒ = λ 1 ​ ℒ mag + λ 2 ​ ℒ phase vel + λ 3 ​ ℒ phase q + λ 4 ​ ℒ IPD + λ 5 ​ ℒ rec . \\mathcal{L}=\\lambda_{1}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mag}}+\\lambda_{2}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{vel}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{3}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{4}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IPD}}+\\lambda_{5}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rec}}. (12) The weights λ ∙ \\lambda_{\\bullet} follow a schedule across training stages (early stages prioritize velocity learning stability; later stages emphasize phase quality terms); specific values are given in the implementation details. (a) Direct baselines (no source-target adaptation). (b) Source-target adapted baselines (marked † ). Figure 2: FOA waveforms (W/Y/Z/X) for a Spatial LibriSpeech test sample; rows share an amplitude scale. Table 2: Spatial audio fidelity and spatial accuracy on Spatial LibriSpeech (cross-room); lower is better. Methods marked † are baselines adapted to the source-to-target setting. ImmerseDiffusion codec reconstruction is analyzed in Appendix B.2 . Setting Method Fidelity Spatial accuracy Wave L2 ↓ \\downarrow Amp. L2 ↓ \\downarrow Phase L2 ↓ \\downarrow MRSTFT ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} ↓ \\downarrow Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} ↓ \\downarrow GT GT reference (estimators on GT waveforms) – – – – 0.139 0.127 0.208 Direct Tango (Ghosal et al. 2023 ) 0.027 2.111 0.730 3.059 1.540 0.511 1.555 ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) 0.013 1.007 0.994 2.905 1.523 0.592 1.507 Diff-SAGe (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) 0.035 1.197 0.685 2.984 0.261 0.280 0.425 Adapted Tango † (mel + BigVGAN) 0.034 1.057 1.545 1.654 1.542 0.461 1.557 Diff-SAGe † 0.020 0.550 1.363 2.214 0.455 0.218 0.534 Ours RIPPLE (GL) 0.022 0.460 1.072 1.318 1.591 0.395 1.586 RIPPLE 0.019 0.432 0.619 1.249 0.243 0.152 0.319 Training and Inference Training regresses both velocities under the combined objective (Eq. ( 12 )). At inference, the two ODEs are integrated sequentially (Fig. 1 ): we first integrate the magnitude flow from log ⁡ | X ( s ) | \\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}| to a predicted target magnitude log ⁡ | X ^ ( t ) | \\log|\\hat{X}^{(\\mathrm{t})}| , then construct the source-informed Griffin–Lim prior p 0 p_{0} from the predicted magnitude m ^ \\hat{m} and the source phase ∠ ​ X ( s ) \\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})} (Eq. 3 ), and integrate the phase ODE from p 0 p_{0} . Magnitude enters the phase stage at two points—the prior construction and the channel-wise conditioning of the phase head—and at inference the predicted m ^ \\hat{m} replaces the oracle | X ( t ) | |X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| at both. This substitution is the only train/inference mismatch in the pipeline. Pseudocode and integration details are in Appendix D.1 . Experimental Setup Datasets and pairs. Primary: Spatial LibriSpeech (Sarabia et al. 2023 ) , 658.7 h of FOA speech; each utterance is rendered in one of 8,000+ synthetic rooms (4 ch, 16 kHz) with source-position and room metadata. Cross-room pairs—two renderings of the same utterance—yield 79,232 training and ∼ {\\sim} 111K test directional pairs. Secondary: SCEDC (SCEDC 2013 ) , following the HEGGS protocol (Jung et al. 2025 ) (60s Z/N/E waveforms at 100Hz, 4,016 train / 861 test events, cross-station pairs from the same event), with the bandpass widened to 0.1–45 Hz to capture low-frequency ground motion. Details in Appendix B.1 . Baselines. In the spatial-audio domain, we compare against two native spatial generators—ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) and Diff-SAGe (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) —and Tango (Ghosal et al. 2023 ) with per-channel BigVGAN vocoding (Lee et al. 2023 ) . Because these are not built for source-to-target translation, we also train source-adapted variants of Diff-SAGe and Tango (marked † ), conditioned on the source spectrogram and trained on our pairs (Appendix B.2 ); these isolate whether source conditioning alone transports inter-channel phase. To separate learned phase from classical recovery directly, RIPPLE (GL) keeps our magnitude head but replaces the phase head with standard random-initialized Griffin–Lim, as used in recovery-based pipelines (Jung et al. 2025 ) ; RIPPLE is the complete model. In the seismic domain, we compare against HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) and a GAN baseline (Wang et al. 2021 ) . Table 3: Cross-station translation on SCEDC (random-direction PAE: 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} ; metric definitions in Appendix B.4 .). Method Goodness-of-fit (GOF) ↑ \\uparrow Inter-component particle motion ↓ \\downarrow PGA PSA FAS Env. I A I_{A} D S D_{S} Mean PAE RLE PAE P RLE P PAE S RLE S GAN (Wang et al. 2021 ) 19.17 22.14 22.78 26.70 9.77 19.46 20.00 58.39 0.287 57.54 0.379 59.13 0.366 HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) 36.41 40.06 36.84 85.74 31.48 68.56 49.85 50.21 0.179 34.58 0.178 54.98 0.173 RIPPLE (GL) 81.96 53.05 47.61 85.61 41.20 65.64 62.51 49.79 0.169 40.42 0.181 51.87 0.153 RIPPLE 90.56 55.86 47.01 85.75 67.73 65.85 68.79 32.57 0.111 26.48 0.121 33.81 0.098 Metrics. Our central metrics measure inter-channel coherence directly; we report standard fidelity metrics alongside them to show the contrast our analysis predicts. In the spatial-audio domain, coherence is captured by spatial accuracy (following ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) ): azimuth L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} , elevation L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} , and an overall Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} —which, since the intensity vector derives from the phase–amplitude alignment between W and Y/Z/X, directly measures the inter-channel coherence a downstream localizer consumes. Alongside these we report waveform/spectral fidelity (Wave L2, Amplitude L2, Phase L2, MRSTFT) following BinauralGrad (Leng et al. 2022 ) . In the seismic domain, the goodness-of-fit (GOF) protocol of (Olsen and Mayhew 2010 ) —per-component scores in [0, 100] over Z/N/E across six intensity measures—is amplitude-based, so our coherence metric is polarization analysis (Jurkevics 1988 ) : polarization angle error (PAE) and rectilinearity L1 error (RLE), reported also in windows at the TauP-predicted P/S arrivals (PAE P , PAE S ). Definitions in Appendix B.4 . Results First-order Ambisonics Domain Table 2 reports results on Spatial LibriSpeech; Figure 2(a) shows representative waveforms. RIPPLE achieves the best scores on all phase-sensitive and spatial metrics. Notably, ImmerseDiffusion attains the lowest Wave L2 (0.013) while its azimuth error sits at the chance level ( 1.523 1.523 vs. π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 )—the starkest instance of the metric insensitivity our analysis predicts: a model can lead the table on waveform fidelity while carrying no directional information. This is not a training failure but a ceiling imposed upstream: its codec’s reconstruction alone already lands azimuth at chance ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.526 L1_{\\theta}{=}1.526 ; Appendix B.2 ), and the full model coincides with that limit (1.523)—no generation in this latent can localize better than the codec permits. Against Diff-SAGe—the strongest direct baseline—the overall spatial error Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} improves from 0.425 to 0.319, a 25% relative reduction. Phase head ablation: Generation beats recovery. Holding the magnitude path fixed, we replace the phase processing with each of two alternatives. (a) Griffin–Lim phase recovery raises Phase L2 from 0.619 0.619 to 1.072 1.072 and Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} from 0.319 0.319 to 1.586 1.586 , a fivefold regression on spatial accuracy. (b) A vocoder-based pipeline fares no better: Tango † , which renders its own predicted mel through BigVGAN, yields 1.545 and 1.557, respectively—worse than Griffin–Lim on phase fidelity despite a strong neural vocoder. This comparison varies the magnitude path as well as the phase path, so it is not a controlled phase-head ablation like (a). Source-target adaptation alone does not transport phase. Adapting Diff-SAGe to the source-target setting (Diff-SAGe † ) sharply improves its fidelity numbers (Wave L2 0.035 → 0.020 0.035\\to 0.020 , Amp. L2 1.197 → 0.550 1.197\\to 0.550 ) but degrades its Phase L2 ( 0.685 → 1.363 0.685\\to 1.363 ): conditioning a complex-spectrogram model on the source spectrogram is not by itself sufficient to transport inter-channel phase coherence to the target (Fig. 2(b) ). RIPPLE improves over Diff-SAGe † on every metric (Table 2 ). Seismology Domai", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false, "persistedOutputPath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/adb0bb30-86c7-403b-bcca-205f6e39bd4b/tool-results/b0qt0c0f5.txt", "persistedOutputSize": 81649}}
