​ ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) , \\displaystyle=(1-t_{p})\\,p_{0}+t_{p}\\big(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\big), (6) v phase ∗ \\displaystyle v^{*}_{\\text{phase}} = ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) − p 0 . \\displaystyle=\\big(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\big)-p_{0}. The timesteps are sampled independently, t m , t p ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0 , 1 ) t_{m},t_{p}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0,1) . We use two separate velocity networks v θ mag v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta} and v θ phase v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta} that share the same conditioning encoder E c ​ ( c ) E_{c}(c) . Since magnitude is informative for phase, the input to v θ phase v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta} additionally concatenates log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| along the channel dimension, making the target magnitude available to the phase head throughout integration. Why decoupled timesteps. Since the two velocity networks share E c E_{c} , tying t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} correlates their training noise levels; independent sampling decorrelates them, and the effect concentrates on inter-channel coherence (Table 4 ). Why a Griffin–Lim prior helps. Starting from p 0 p_{0} rather than noise places the phase flow near the STFT-consistency manifold at the target magnitude, so it learns only a residual correction—shortening transport and letting short schedules suffice (Figure 4 ). Training Objective The overall training objective is a weighted sum of two velocity-regression losses, a magnitude-reconstruction loss, phase-quality terms, and an inter-channel phase-difference (IPD) loss. All phase-related losses are magnitude-weighted so that low-energy bins, where phase is informationally degenerate, do not dominate training. For convenience, we define the one-step prediction of each modality ∙ ∈ { mag , phase } \\bullet\\in\\{\\mathrm{mag},\\mathrm{phase}\\} as z ^ 1 | t ∙ = z t ∙ + ( 1 − t ) ​ v θ ∙ ​ ( ⋅ ) , \\hat{z}^{\\bullet}_{1|t}=z^{\\bullet}_{t}+(1-t)\\,v^{\\bullet}_{\\theta}(\\cdot), (7) Magnitude-aware weighting. We weight phase losses by a per-sample normalized sigmoid gate w ​ ( | X ( t ) | ) = σ ​ ( log ⁡ | X ( t ) | − μ s ) , w(|X^{(t)}|)=\\sigma\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log|X^{(t)}|-\\mu}{s}\\right), (8) where μ \\mu and s s are the per-channel mean and standard deviation of log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(t)}| over the ( f , t ) (f,t) axes of each sample, and w w is subsequently normalized to unit mean per channel; low-magnitude bins gracefully fall back to the prior. Velocity losses. For the two modalities, ℒ mag \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mag}} = 𝔼 ​ [ ‖ v θ mag − v mag ∗ ‖ 2 ] , \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\|v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta}-v^{*}_{\\text{mag}}\\|^{2}\\right], (9) ℒ phase vel \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{vel}}_{\\text{phase}} = 𝔼 ​ [ w ​ ( | X ( t ) | ) ​ ‖ v θ phase − v phase ∗ ‖ 2 ] . \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[w(|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|)\\,\\|v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta}-v^{*}_{\\text{phase}}\\|^{2}\\right]. (10) Inter-channel phase consistency (IPD) loss. The losses above are all channel-wise, so inter-channel coherence would only emerge as a byproduct. To make it an explicit training signal, we project predictions onto 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} by unit normalization, compute relative phase vectors r i ​ j = e i ​ ( ϕ i − ϕ j ) r_{ij}=e^{i(\\phi_{i}-\\phi_{j})} for each channel pair ( i , j ) (i,j) from both the prediction and the target, and maximize their inner product: ℒ IPD = 1 | 𝒫 | ​ ∑ ( i , j ) ∈ 𝒫 𝔼 ​ [ w i ​ j ​ ( 1 − ⟨ r ^ i ​ j , r i ​ j ∗ ⟩ ) ] , \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IPD}}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{P}|}\\!\\!\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{P}}\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[w_{ij}\\big(1-\\langle\\hat{r}_{ij},\\,r^{*}_{ij}\\rangle\\big)\\right], (11) where 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} is the set of channel pairs, ⟨ ⋅ , ⋅ ⟩ \\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle is the inner product of the two-dimensional unit vectors ( = cos ⁡ ( Δ ​ IPD ) =\\cos(\\Delta\\text{IPD}) ), and w i ​ j = σ ​ ( ( min ⁡ ( log ⁡ m i , log ⁡ m j ) − μ ) / s ) w_{ij}=\\sigma\\!\\bigl((\\min(\\log m_{i},\\log m_{j})-\\mu)/s\\bigr) applies the gating of (Eq. 8 ) with the element-wise minimum of the two channels’ log-magnitudes in place of log ⁡ | X ( t ) | \\log|X^{(t)}| , so a bin contributes only where both channels are energetic. This loss is invariant to a common rotation of ϕ i \\phi_{i} and ϕ j \\phi_{j} (global phase shift invariance), matching the invariance of the physical quantities we evaluate (DOA and polarization direction) and thereby placing the learning signal directly on inter-channel structure. Auxiliary losses. Velocity regression alone enforces neither the unit-circle constraint nor endpoint consistency. From the one-step phase prediction z ^ 1 | t p phase = ( c ^ , s ^ ) \\hat{z}^{\\text{phase}}_{1|t_{p}}=(\\hat{c},\\hat{s}) we add ℒ phase q \\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}} , a weighted sum of a phase direction loss, an instantaneous-frequency loss on ∂ t ϕ \\partial_{t}\\phi , and a unit-norm penalty ( c ^ 2 + s ^ 2 − 1 ) 2 (\\hat{c}^{2}+\\hat{s}^{2}-1)^{2} ; and ℒ rec = 𝔼 ​ [ ‖ z ^ 1 | t m mag − log ⁡ | X ( t ) | ‖ 2 ] \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rec}}=\\mathbb{E}[\\|\\hat{z}^{\\text{mag}}_{1|t_{m}}-\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|\\|^{2}] , which reweights the magnitude velocity error by ( 1 − t m ) 2 (1-t_{m})^{2} to emphasize early timesteps. Definitions in App. B.3 . Total loss. The overall objective is ℒ = λ 1 ​ ℒ mag + λ 2 ​ ℒ phase vel + λ 3 ​ ℒ phase q + λ 4 ​ ℒ IPD + λ 5 ​ ℒ rec . \\mathcal{L}=\\lambda_{1}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mag}}+\\lambda_{2}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{vel}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{3}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{4}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IPD}}+\\lambda_{5}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rec}}. (12) The weights λ ∙ \\lambda_{\\bullet} follow a schedule across training stages (early stages prioritize velocity learning stability; later stages emphasize phase quality terms); specific values are given in the implementation details. (a) Direct baselines (no source-target adaptation). (b) Source-target adapted baselines (marked † ). Figure 2: FOA waveforms (W/Y/Z/X) for a Spatial LibriSpeech test sample; rows share an amplitude scale. Table 2: Spatial audio fidelity and spatial accuracy on Spatial LibriSpeech (cross-room); lower is better. Methods marked † are baselines adapted to the source-to-target setting. ImmerseDiffusion codec reconstruction is analyzed in Appendix B.2 . Setting Method Fidelity Spatial accuracy Wave L2 ↓ \\downarrow Amp. L2 ↓ \\downarrow Phase L2 ↓ \\downarrow MRSTFT ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} ↓ \\downarrow Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} ↓ \\downarrow GT GT reference (estimators on GT waveforms) – – – – 0.139 0.127 0.208 Direct Tango (Ghosal et al. 2023 ) 0.027 2.111 0.730 3.059 1.540 0.511 1.555 ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) 0.013 1.007 0.994 2.905 1.523 0.592 1.507 Diff-SAGe (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) 0.035 1.197 0.685 2.984 0.261 0.280 0.425 Adapted Tango † (mel + BigVGAN) 0.034 1.057 1.545 1.654 1.542 0.461 1.557 Diff-SAGe † 0.020 0.550 1.363 2.214 0.455 0.218 0.534 Ours RIPPLE (GL) 0.022 0.460 1.072 1.318 1.591 0.395 1.586 RIPPLE 0.019 0.432 0.619 1.249 0.243 0.152 0.319 Training and Inference Training regresses both velocities under the combined objective (Eq. ( 12 )). At inference, the two ODEs are integrated sequentially (Fig. 1 ): we first integrate the magnitude flow from log ⁡ | X ( s ) | \\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}| to a predicted target magnitude log ⁡ | X ^ ( t ) | \\log|\\hat{X}^{(\\mathrm{t})}| , then construct the source-informed Griffin–Lim prior p 0 p_{0} from the predicted magnitude m ^ \\hat{m} and the source phase ∠ ​ X ( s ) \\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})} (Eq. 3 ), and integrate the phase ODE from p 0 p_{0} . Magnitude enters the phase stage at two points—the prior construction and the channel-wise conditioning of the phase head—and at inference the predicted m ^ \\hat{m} replaces the oracle | X ( t ) | |X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| at both. This substitution is the only train/inference mismatch in the pipeline. Pseudocode and integration details are in Appendix D.1 . Experimental Setup Datasets and pairs. Primary: Spatial LibriSpeech (Sarabia et al. 2023 ) , 658.7 h of FOA speech; each utterance is rendered in one of 8,000+ synthetic rooms (4 ch, 16 kHz) with source-position and room metadata. Cross-room pairs—two renderings of the same utterance—yield 79,232 training and ∼ {\\sim} 111K test directional pairs. Secondary: SCEDC (SCEDC 2013 ) , following the HEGGS protocol (Jung et al. 2025 ) (60s Z/N/E waveforms at 100Hz, 4,016 train / 861 test events, cross-station pairs from the same event), with the bandpass widened to 0.1–45 Hz to capture low-frequency ground motion. Details in Appendix B.1 . Baselines. In the spatial-audio domain, we compare against two native spatial generators—ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) and Diff-SAGe (Kushwaha et al. 2025 ) —and Tango (Ghosal et al. 2023 ) with per-channel BigVGAN vocoding (Lee et al. 2023 ) . Because these are not built for source-to-target translation, we also train source-adapted variants of Diff-SAGe and Tango (marked † ), conditioned on the source spectrogram and trained on our pairs (Appendix B.2 ); these isolate whether source conditioning alone transports inter-channel phase. To separate learned phase from classical recovery directly, RIPPLE (GL) keeps our magnitude head but replaces the phase head with standard random-initialized Griffin–Lim, as used in recovery-based pipelines (Jung et al. 2025 ) ; RIPPLE is the complete model. In the seismic domain, we compare against HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) and a GAN baseline (Wang et al. 2021 ) . Table 3: Cross-station translation on SCEDC (random-direction PAE: 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} ; metric definitions in Appendix B.4 .). Method Goodness-of-fit (GOF) ↑ \\uparrow Inter-component particle motion ↓ \\downarrow PGA PSA FAS Env. I A I_{A} D S D_{S} Mean PAE RLE PAE P RLE P PAE S RLE S GAN (Wang et al. 2021 ) 19.17 22.14 22.78 26.70 9.77 19.46 20.00 58.39 0.287 57.54 0.379 59.13 0.366 HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) 36.41 40.06 36.84 85.74 31.48 68.56 49.85 50.21 0.179 34.58 0.178 54.98 0.173 RIPPLE (GL) 81.96 53.05 47.61 85.61 41.20 65.64 62.51 49.79 0.169 40.42 0.181 51.87 0.153 RIPPLE 90.56 55.86 47.01 85.75 67.73 65.85 68.79 32.57 0.111 26.48 0.121 33.81 0.098 Metrics. Our central metrics measure inter-channel coherence directly; we report standard fidelity metrics alongside them to show the contrast our analysis predicts. In the spatial-audio domain, coherence is captured by spatial accuracy (following ImmerseDiffusion (Heydari et al. 2025 ) ): azimuth L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} , elevation L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} , and an overall Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} —which, since the intensity vector derives from the phase–amplitude alignment between W and Y/Z/X, directly measures the inter-channel coherence a downstream localizer consumes. Alongside these we report waveform/spectral fidelity (Wave L2, Amplitude L2, Phase L2, MRSTFT) following BinauralGrad (Leng et al. 2022 ) . In the seismic domain, the goodness-of-fit (GOF) protocol of (Olsen and Mayhew 2010 ) —per-component scores in [0, 100] over Z/N/E across six intensity measures—is amplitude-based, so our coherence metric is polarization analysis (Jurkevics 1988 ) : polarization angle error (PAE) and rectilinearity L1 error (RLE), reported also in windows at the TauP-predicted P/S arrivals (PAE P , PAE S ). Definitions in Appendix B.4 . Results First-order Ambisonics Domain Table 2 reports results on Spatial LibriSpeech; Figure 2(a) shows representative waveforms. RIPPLE achieves the best scores on all phase-sensitive and spatial metrics. Notably, ImmerseDiffusion attains the lowest Wave L2 (0.013) while its azimuth error sits at the chance level ( 1.523 1.523 vs. π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 )—the starkest instance of the metric insensitivity our analysis predicts: a model can lead the table on waveform fidelity while carrying no directional information. This is not a training failure but a ceiling imposed upstream: its codec’s reconstruction alone already lands azimuth at chance ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.526 L1_{\\theta}{=}1.526 ; Appendix B.2 ), and the full model coincides with that limit (1.523)—no generation in this latent can localize better than the codec permits. Against Diff-SAGe—the strongest direct baseline—the overall spatial error Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} improves from 0.425 to 0.319, a 25% relative reduction. Phase head ablation: Generation beats recovery. Holding the magnitude path fixed, we replace the phase processing with each of two alternatives. (a) Griffin–Lim phase recovery raises Phase L2 from 0.619 0.619 to 1.072 1.072 and Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} from 0.319 0.319 to 1.586 1.586 , a fivefold regression on spatial accuracy. (b) A vocoder-based pipeline fares no better: Tango † , which renders its own predicted mel through BigVGAN, yields 1.545 and 1.557, respectively—worse than Griffin–Lim on phase fidelity despite a strong neural vocoder. This comparison varies the magnitude path as well as the phase path, so it is not a controlled phase-head ablation like (a). Source-target adaptation alone does not transport phase. Adapting Diff-SAGe to the source-target setting (Diff-SAGe † ) sharply improves its fidelity numbers (Wave L2 0.035 → 0.020 0.035\\to 0.020 , Amp. L2 1.197 → 0.550 1.197\\to 0.550 ) but degrades its Phase L2 ( 0.685 → 1.363 0.685\\to 1.363 ): conditioning a complex-spectrogram model on the source spectrogram is not by itself sufficient to transport inter-channel phase coherence to the target (Fig. 2(b) ). RIPPLE improves over Diff-SAGe † on every metric (Table 2 ). Seismology Domain Figure 3: Three-component (Z/N/E) log-magnitude spectrograms for an SCEDC cross-station test sample. Table 3 reports cross-station translation on SCEDC, where all methods share the HEGGS conditioning protocol (Appendix B.2 ) so the comparison isolates architecture. RIPPLE attains a mean GOF of 68.79 (\"good\" on the Olsen–Mayhew scale) against 49.85 (\"fair\") for HEGGS. The advantage concentrates on what HEGGS’s compressed VAE latent cannot preserve—peak amplitude and energy content (PGA 36.41 → 90.56 36.41\\to 90.56 , I A I_{A} 31.48 → 67.73 31.48\\to 67.73 ), while the two are tied on envelope-level quantities (Env. 85.74 vs. 85.75); Figure 3 and Appendix E trace this to latent compression, and FAS (47.01) remains our lowest score. Table 4: Ablation on both domains, all else fixed (random-direction PAE: 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} ; lower is better except GOF). Full metrics and row definitions in Appendix C . Spatial audio Seismic Configuration Ph. L2 L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} GOF ↑ \\uparrow PAE PAE S RIPPLE 0.619 0.243 0.152 68.79 32.57 33.81 w/o src-coherent init 0.683 0.253 0.161 65.13 43.28 48.23 w/o GL prior 1.404 1.616 0.615 59.39 52.07 57.33 w/o IPD ( λ 4 = 0 \\lambda_{4}{=}0 ) 1.451 1.627 0.188 60.04 52.38 57.31 coupled t t ( t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} ) 0.807 1.582 0.200 57.84 50.98 50.28 RIPPLE (GL) 1.072 1.591 0.395 62.51 49.79 51.87 prior only (no flow) 0.717 1.459 0.291 62.87 45.17 44.58 Inter-component particle motion. The GOF above is amplitude-based; our central claim is tested only by polarization, and there the result is sharpest. S-wave polarization rides on the relative phase between horizontal components—exactly what per-channel recovery cannot produce—and every recovery-based method collapses to chance: GAN 59.1 ∘ 59.1^{\\circ} , HEGGS 55.0 ∘ 55.0^{\\circ} , RIPPLE (GL) 51.9 ∘ 51.9^{\\circ} , against a 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} random expectation. RIPPLE alone preserves it, at 33.8 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ} . The contrast is mechanistic, not architectural: HEGGS and RIPPLE (GL) reach nearly identical polarization errors despite entirely different generators (LDM likewise; App. B.2 ) because they share per-channel Griffin–Lim recovery—learning the phase is what breaks the ceiling ( 49.8 ∘ → 32.6 ∘ 49.8^{\\circ}\\!\\to\\!32.6^{\\circ} , RLE 0.169 → 0.111 0.169\\to 0.111 ). The P/S split supports the mechanism: P-wave polarization is partly constrained by source–receiver geometry and thus recoverable from magnitudes (HEGGS 34.6 ∘ 34.6^{\\circ} ), while S-wave polarization is not—only learned phase recovers it (GOF-level corroboration in Appendix C ). Ablation Studies Table 4 removes one component at a time from RIPPLE on both domains, keeping the magnitude path and all other settings fixed (full metrics in Appendix C ). A structured prior determines feasibility. Replacing the Griffin–Lim prior with a Gaussian collapses both domains to their no-learning baselines: in the spatial-audio domain, Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} regresses from 0.319 0.319 to 1.604 1.604 (the level of classical Griffin–Lim recovery), and in the seismic domain, PAE S lands at the random-direction expectation ( 57.33 ∘ 57.33^{\\circ} vs. 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} ). Without a structured starting point, and this holds at every integration budget (Fig. 4 ). Figure 4: Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} vs. integration steps N N on Spatial LibriSpeech (100 test pairs; 4N network evaluations total, 2N per Heun stage). Dashed line: classical Griffin–Lim recovery. Source coherence matters where magnitudes cannot reach. The endpoint contribution of source-coherent initialization differs across domains, validating our analysis. In the spatial-audio domain, removing it barely moves the endpoint ( 0.319 → 0.332 0.319\\to 0.332 ); its role is few-step efficiency (at N = 2 N{=}2 it already beats the randomly initialized prior at N = 25 N{=}25 ; Figure 4 ). In the seismic domain, the endpoint regresses (PAE 32.6 ∘ → 43.3 ∘ 32.6^{\\circ}\\to 43.3^{\\circ} ), and the degradation is concentrated exactly where phase is unrecoverable from magnitudes: P-wave polarization degrades by only 6.7 ∘ 6.7^{\\circ} ( 26.5 ∘ → 33.2 ∘ 26.5^{\\circ}\\to 33.2^{\\circ} ), while S-wave polarization degrades by more than twice as much ( 33.8 ∘ → 48.2 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ}\\to 48.2^{\\circ} ). Even without the flow, the prior alone already sits well below chance on S-wave polarization (PAE S 44.6 ∘ 44.6^{\\circ} vs. 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} ; Table 4 , prior only )—initialization preserves exactly what the transfer functions preserve, and no more. Cross-channel coherence requires both the IPD objective and decoupled timesteps. Setting λ 4 = 0 \\lambda_{4}{=}0 leaves elevation nearly intact ( 0.152 → 0.188 0.152\\to 0.188 ) but collapses cross-channel structure: azimuth collapses ( L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} 0.243 → 1.627 0.243\\to 1.627 ), and in the seismic domain, PAE S again reaches the random level ( 57.31 ∘ 57.31^{\\circ} )—channel-wise velocity losses alone do not yield coherence as a byproduct. Coupling the timesteps ( t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} ) exposes the same fault line through a different mechanism: per-bin phase is largely spared (Phase L2 0.619 → 0.807 0.619\\to 0.807 ) while inter-channel coherence collapses ( Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} 0.319 → 1.581 0.319\\to 1.581 ), consistent with the noise-level correlation induced by the shared encoder when t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} . Table 5: Oracle reconstruction of ground-truth targets—no generation, no magnitude error. Fidelity survives every recovery route; the coherence metric does not. GT estimator floor: L ​ 1 θ = 0.139 L1_{\\theta}=0.139 . Full results in Appendix B.2 . Recovery route Coherence Fidelity FOA L ​ 1 θ ↓ L1_{\\theta}\\downarrow [chance 1.571] Wave L2 ↓ \\downarrow Griffin–Lim, GT magnitudes 1.567 0.013 BigVGAN round trip 1.607 0.035 ImmerseDiffusion codec 1.526 0.011 Seismic PAE ↓ S {}_{\\mathrm{S}}\\downarrow [chance 57.3 ∘ ] GOF ↑ \\uparrow VAE round trip + + GT phase 3.7 ∘ 3.7^{\\circ} 98.4 VAE round trip + + GL phase 42.9 ∘ 42.9^{\\circ} 95.3 Discussion One might attribute the gains to Griffin–Lim merely supplying a better magnitude-conditioned starting point rather than to inter-channel structure. The ablation ladder separates the two: randomizing the prior’s initialization while keeping Griffin–Lim isolates the source-coherent contribution, and the further collapse under a Gaussian prior isolates magnitude consistency (Table 4 , Figure 4 ). A single distinction remains: the Gaussian replacement removes magnitude consistency and valid 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} structure together. The K = 0 K=0 prior of Appendix C.1 —the source phase with no magnitude projection—partially separates them and lands far closer to the full model than to the Gaussian level; a flow trained with such a prior would settle it. The bottleneck is the recovery stage, not the generator. Table 5 removes the generator from the question: every row reconstructs ground-truth targets, so any failure is due to recovery alone. Fidelity survives every route while the quantity that downstream analyses consume collapses: azimuth reaches chance on all three spatial routes, and replacing ground-truth phase with recovery degrades S-wave polarization by an order of magnitude. The ceilings bind tightly—a latent-codec generator’s spatial accuracy coincides with its own codec’s limit (1.523 vs. 1.526), and three architecturally distinct seismic generators sharing per-channel recovery land within a few degrees ( 55.0 ∘ , 51.9 ∘ , 56.8 ∘ 55.0^{\\circ},51.9^{\\circ},56.8^{\\circ} ). Nor is it a magnitude problem: recovery from near-oracle magnitudes ( 42.9 ∘ 42.9^{\\circ} ) stays worse than RIPPLE on its own predicted ones ( 33.8 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ} ). A recovery-based pipeline inherits the ceiling of its recovery stage; a better generator cannot lift it, but learned phase can. Limitations. First, the framework presumes that source and target share inter-channel structure through a common source signal. When this assumption weakens—as in low-SNR recordings, or in pairs whose transfer functions destroy the shared structure—the prior degenerates toward a randomly initialized one, and the w/o source-coherent init ablation (Table 4 ) measures the cost: efficiency loss where the target’s structure is recoverable from magnitudes, and endpoint loss where it is not (Figure 4 ). Second, our evaluation is objective (DOA, polarization): FOA is not a playback format, so a listening test would entangle inter-channel structure with the binaural renderer and HRTF (Wenzel et al. 1993 ) . Finally, K K trades prior quality against compute; we train at a fixed K K and show inference-time robustness only (Appendix C.1 ). Source-free tasks such as text-to-spatial generation remain future work. Conclusion Multi-channel waveforms carry their physical content—source direction in ambisonics, particle motion in seismograms—in the relationships between channels, yet existing generative AI methods have delegated phase to channel-independent recovery. We show that this delegation carries a measurable cost, and offer a practical remedy: reinterpreting Griffin–Lim as a source-coherent prior for a decoupled rectified flow turns phase into a generation target without discarding what classical estimation provides. This approach improves empirical performance on first-order ambisonics environment transfers and seismic cross-station translations—most sharply on S-wave polarization, which per-channel reconstruction leaves near chance level. We hope this reframing— generating multi-channel phase, not recovering it —encourages treating inter-channel coherence as a first-class generation target, tracked alongside magnitude-based fidelity. References M. S. Albergo and E. Vanden-Eijnden (2023) Building normalizing flows with stochastic interpolants . In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) , Cited by: Flow matching and rectified flows for audio. . F. Bao, S. Nie, K. Xue, C. Li, S. Pu, Y. Wang, G. Yue, Y. Cao, H. Su, and J. Zhu (2023) One transformer fits all distributions in multi-modal diffusion at scale . In Inte