 through two transfer functions. SCEDC (cross-station, HEGGS-compatible). As in HEGGS, we sample two stations of the same event as ( s , t ) (\\mathrm{s},\\mathrm{t}) and define dataset size by the number of events, so per-epoch iterations scale with events rather than all pairs. Following the HEGGS pairing scheme (two stations of one event as source/target), we rebuild the split from the SCEDC catalog, yielding 4,016 train / 861 test events. B.2 Baseline adaptation. Spatial Baselines. Diff-SAGe and Tango are not designed for source-to-target translation; we extend each by additionally conditioning the generator on the source spectrogram, trained on the same pair construction as our model (variants marked † ). ImmerseDiffusion codec and full model. We reproduced the ImmerseDiffusion FOA codec—the DAC autoencoder (Kumar et al. 2023 ) with its RVQ bottleneck replaced by a continuous VAE, at 128x compression, following (Heydari et al. 2025 ) —and evaluated both its reconstruction (autoencoding round trip, no generation) and the full generative model trained on our cross-room protocol, on the same Spatial LibriSpeech test targets as Table 2 (Table 7 ). Codec reconstruction alone lands azimuth near the chance level: L ​ 1 θ = 1.526 L1_{\\theta}=1.526 , where π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 is the expected circular L ​ 1 L1 error of any azimuth estimate independent of the target (estimator floor on GT waveforms: 0.139 0.139 ); the original paper’s published variants report 1.43 1.43 – 1.57 1.57 on Spatial AudioCaps, consistent with this level. Notably, ImmerseDiffusion trains its FOA codec with per-channel MR-STFT losses, explicitly arguing that channel orthogonality removes the need for a cross-channel objective, and its codec reconstruction alone lands azimuth at chance ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.526 L1_{\\theta}=1.526 ), a direct consequence of that channel-independent design. Elevation, partially recoverable from channel energy ratios, degrades but survives the round trip ( L ​ 1 φ = 0.590 L1_{\\varphi}=0.590 vs. floor 0.127 0.127 )—the codec-level counterpart of the magnitude-recoverable/phase-dependent split between P- and S-wave polarization (Table 3 ). The full model then confirms this bound is tight: its spatial accuracy ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.523 L1_{\\theta}=1.523 , Δ SA = 1.508 \\Delta_{\\text{SA}}=1.508 ) coincides with its own codec’s reconstruction limit ( 1.526 1.526 , 1.512 1.512 ). The generator is not failing to learn; the codec caps what any generation in its latent space can localize. For the same reason we do not train a source-adapted ImmerseDiffusion † : source conditioning enters before the codec and cannot lift this ceiling. Table 7: ImmerseDiffusion FOA codec reconstruction (autoencoding round trip, no generation): published results vs. our reproduction. Upper block quoted from Heydari et al. ( 2025 ) (evaluated on Spatial AudioCaps; trained on their full data mixture); lower block is our reproduction of the 128 × \\times codec, trained and evaluated on Spatial LibriSpeech only (Table 2 ). Estimator floors are dataset-specific, so absolute values are comparable within a block; the azimuth chance level is not— π / 2 \\pi/2 is the expected circular L ​ 1 L1 error of any azimuth estimate independent of the target. All published variants sit at or near this level, as does our reproduction. Lower is better. Eval set Model L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} Δ SA \\Delta_{\\text{SA}} Spatial AudioCaps (quoted) 1D-Conv U-Net (32 × \\times ) 1.55 0.29 1.56 1D-Conv U-Net (64 × \\times ) 1.57 0.24 1.57 1D-Conv U-Net (128 × \\times ) 1.57 0.16 1.57 1D-Conv U-Net- β \\beta (128 × \\times ) 1.43 0.15 1.51 Measurement error (floor) 0.17 0.14 1.03 Spatial LibriSpeech(ours) Reproduced codec (128 × \\times ) 1.526 0.590 1.512 GT estimator floor 0.139 0.127 0.208 — Chance (target-independent azimuth) π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 – – Table 8: Oracle reconstruction of ground-truth FOA waveforms (no generation): per-channel Griffin–Lim on ground-truth magnitudes, and BigVGAN per-channel mel round trip. Spatial LibriSpeech test targets; lower is better. Wave L2 ↓ \\downarrow Amp. L2 ↓ \\downarrow Phase L2 ↓ \\downarrow MRSTFT ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} Δ SA \\Delta_{\\text{SA}} Griffin–Lim (GT magnitudes) 0.013 0.007 1.553 0.552 1.567 0.402 1.560 BigVGAN reconstruction 0.035 0.544 1.552 1.575 1.607 0.384 1.606 GT estimator floor – – – – 0.139 0.127 0.208 Chance (target-indep. azimuth) – – – – π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 – – Griffin–Lim on ground-truth magnitudes. The simplest recovery route sets the ceiling most cleanly: we run per-channel Griffin–Lim on the ground-truth target magnitudes (no generation, no magnitude error) and evaluate the reconstruction (Table 8 ). Magnitude-side fidelity is near-perfect by construction (Wave L2 0.013, Amp. L2 0.007)—matching the best waveform fidelity any model attains in Table 2 —yet azimuth sits at the chance level ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.567 L1_{\\theta}=1.567 vs. π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 ; floor 0.139), while elevation partially survives (0.402 vs. floor 0.127): the same magnitude-recoverable/phase-dependent split as the two routes below, now with all generation and magnitude error removed. Vocoder reconstruction. To isolate the vocoder from generation error, we pass ground-truth FOA waveforms through BigVGAN per channel (mel analysis and re-synthesis, no generation) and evaluate the reconstruction (Table 8 ). Per-channel fidelity survives the round trip (Wave L2 0.035), but azimuth lands at the chance level ( L ​ 1 θ = 1.607 L1_{\\theta}=1.607 vs. π / 2 ≈ 1.571 \\pi/2\\approx 1.571 ; floor 0.139), while elevation partially survives (0.384 vs. floor 0.127)—the same magnitude-recoverable/phase-dependent split as the codec above. The vocoder route is thus capped before any generative model enters: Tango’s adapted variant (Table 2 , L ​ 1 θ = 1.542 L1_{\\theta}=1.542 ) inherits this ceiling rather than failing to learn. Seismic Baselines. All seismic methods follow the conditioning protocol of HEGGS (Jung et al. 2025 ) , whose generator is natively conditioned on the source-station waveform; the GAN and Latent Diffusion Model (LDM) (Rombach et al. 2022 ) baselines are adapted to receive the same source conditioning, as done in the HEGGS paper’s own baseline comparisons. All methods additionally share the HEGGS conditioning set — source longitude/latitude, station longitude/latitude, back azimuth, source–station distance, source depth, and local magnitude — and the HEGGS conditioning protocol, with preprocessing modified to a wider 0.1–45 Hz bandpass (vs. the 1–45 Hz of (Jung et al. 2025 ) ) to retain low-frequency content; all else follows their pipeline (60 s three-component windows at 100 Hz). Latent-diffusion VAE reconstruction (seismic). Table 9: LDM VAE reconstruction of ground-truth SCEDC waveforms (autoencoding round trip, no generation), reassembled with ground-truth phase vs. per-channel Griffin–Lim recovery. Method Goodness-of-fit (GOF) ↑ \\uparrow Inter-component particle motion ↓ \\downarrow PGA PSA FAS Env. I A I_{A} D S D_{S} Mean PAE RLE PAE P RLE P PAE S RLE S VAE recon + GT Phase 95.58 98.57 98.35 99.92 98.62 99.11 98.36 4.10 0.022 2.22 0.011 3.68 0.016 VAE recon + GL Phase 88.30 92.62 94.34 98.50 99.31 98.93 95.33 41.34 0.083 28.05 0.068 42.93 0.075 The latent route on seismic shows the same ceiling as its spatial counterparts. We pass ground-truth target waveforms through the LDM (Rombach et al. 2022 ) baseline’s VAE (autoencoding round trip, no generation) and reassemble with either the ground-truth phase or per-channel Griffin–Lim recovery (Table 9 ). With ground-truth phase, reconstruction is near-perfect on both axes (mean GOF 98.36; PAE S = 3.7 ∘ 3.7^{\\circ} ): the VAE itself is not the bottleneck. Substituting Griffin–Lim recovery barely moves the amplitude-based scores (mean GOF 95.33, still \"excellent\" on the Olsen–Mayhew (Olsen and Mayhew 2010 ) scale) while degrading S-wave polarization by an order of magnitude ( 3.7 ∘ 3.7^{\\circ} → \\to 42.9 ∘ 42.9^{\\circ} )—the seismic counterpart of the metric insensitivity in Tables 7 and 8 , now with all generation error removed. The split between arrivals repeats the pattern of the spatial routes: P-wave polarization, partially constrained by magnitudes, survives recovery far better ( 28.1 ∘ 28.1^{\\circ} ) than S-wave polarization does. Notably, this oracle-magnitude recovery ceiling (PAE S 42.9 ∘ 42.9^{\\circ} ) is still 9.1 ∘ 9.1^{\\circ} worse than RIPPLE operating on its own predicted magnitudes ( 33.8 ∘ 33.8^{\\circ} , Table 3 ): even perfect magnitudes do not rescue per-channel phase recovery. Latent-diffusion generation (seismic). Table 10: Latent-diffusion (LDM) generation on SCEDC cross-station translation, evaluated on the same test targets as Table 3 . Random-direction PAE: 57.3 ∘ . GOF higher is better; particle-motion errors lower is better. Goodness-of-fit (GOF) ↑ \\uparrow Inter-component particle motion ↓ \\downarrow Method PGA PSA FAS Env. I A I_{A} D S D_{S} Mean PAE RLE PAE P RLE P PAE S RLE S LDM (Rombach et al. 2022 ) 39.46 42.70 38.47 84.68 37.07 71.05 52.24 50.02 0.200 35.03 0.169 56.84 0.229 Table 9 isolates the LDM VAE from generation error by round-tripping ground-truth waveforms; here we evaluate the full LDM generator (Rombach et al. 2022 ) trained on our cross-station protocol, on the same test targets as Table 3 (Table 10 ). Its amplitude-based GOF is competitive (mean 52.2, comparable to HEGGS at 49.9), yet its S-wave polarization sits at the random-direction level (PAE S 56.8 ∘ 56.8^{\\circ} vs. 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} )—indistinguishable from chance, and no better than HEGGS ( 55.0 ∘ 55.0^{\\circ} ) or RIPPLE (GL) ( 51.9 ∘ 51.9^{\\circ} ). A third architecture thus reaches the same ceiling: the bottleneck is per-channel phase recovery, not the generative backbone. B.3 Implementation details. Loss schedule. In Eq. 12 we fix λ 1 = λ 2 = λ 5 = 1 \\lambda_{1}=\\lambda_{2}=\\lambda_{5}=1 throughout training, while the phase quality terms are warmed up: λ 3 = λ 4 = min ⁡ ( max ⁡ ( ( e − e 0 ) / Δ ​ e , 0 ) , 1 ) \\lambda_{3}=\\lambda_{4}=\\min\\!\\bigl(\\max\\bigl((e-e_{0})/\\Delta e,\\,0\\bigr),\\,1\\bigr) at epoch e e , ramping linearly from 0 to 1 over epochs 5–15 on Spatial LibriSpeech and epochs 150–200 on SCEDC. Early training thus prioritizes velocity-learning stability; the phase quality and IPD terms take full effect once the velocity fields have stabilized. Phase quality loss. ℒ phase q = 0.1 ​ L dir + 0.5 ​ L IF + 0.1 ​ L unit \\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}}=0.1\\,L_{\\mathrm{dir}}+0.5\\,L_{\\mathrm{IF}}+0.1\\,L_{\\mathrm{unit}} , where (a) L dir L_{\\mathrm{dir}} is the magnitude-weighted cosine-similarity loss between ( c ^ , s ^ ) (\\hat{c},\\hat{s}) and ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) (\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}) ; (b) L IF L_{\\mathrm{IF}} is an instantaneous-frequency loss comparing first-order temporal differences on S 1 S^{1} via the cross product c ^ t ​ s ^ t + 1 − s ^ t ​ c ^ t + 1 ≈ sin ⁡ ( ϕ t + 1 − ϕ t ) \\hat{c}_{t}\\hat{s}_{t+1}-\\hat{s}_{t}\\hat{c}_{t+1}\\approx\\sin(\\phi_{t+1}-\\phi_{t}) , avoiding explicit phase unwrapping, with an L1 penalty against the same quantity computed from the target; and (c) L unit L_{\\mathrm{unit}} is the unit-norm penalty ( c ^ 2 + s ^ 2 − 1 ) 2 (\\hat{c}^{2}+\\hat{s}^{2}-1)^{2} . All three terms are gated by Eq. 8 . Training configuration. Both velocity networks are UNet-based; the prior uses K = 8 K{=}8 Griffin–Lim iterations and σ prior = 0.1 \\sigma_{\\text{prior}}{=}0.1 . All models are trained with AdamW at learning rate 10 − 5 10^{-5} , batch size 4, and gradient accumulation over 8 steps (effective batch size 32). Our model trains for 50 epochs on Spatial LibriSpeech and 300 on SCEDC; all baselines we train or adapt use 300 epochs on their respective domain (Diff-SAGe † and Tango † on spatial; HEGGS, GAN, and LDM on seismic). Spatial experiments run on two NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs, seismic on two RTX A6000s. B.4 Evaluation metrics. Goodness-of-fit (GOF). Per-component scores are GOF = 100 ⋅ erfc ​ ( 2 ​ | x − y | / ( x + y ) ) \\text{GOF}=100\\cdot\\mathrm{erfc}\\big(2|x-y|/(x+y)\\big) (Olsen and Mayhew 2010 ) , computed on six intensity measures: peak ground acceleration (PGA); pseudo-spectral acceleration (PSA, 0.1–10 s, 5% damped); Konno–Ohmachi-smoothed Fourier amplitude spectrum (FAS); Arias intensity ( I A I_{A} ); significant duration ( D S ​ 5 ​ – ​ 95 D_{S5\\text{--}95} ); and envelope correlation (Env.). Polarization metrics. PAE/RLE are computed in sliding windows over the same evaluation interval as the GOF measures: in each window we eigendecompose the 3 × 3 3{\\times}3 covariance of the three components; the principal eigenvector gives the particle-motion direction (compared via the angle between target and prediction, with sign ambiguity resolved by | cos | |\\cos| ) and the eigenvalue ratios give rectilinearity. This is the same analysis practitioners run on real three-component records, so PAE measures usability for the workflows that cross-station translation is meant to serve. Window contributions are weighted by target energy and target rectilinearity, since polarization direction is only well-defined for energetic, rectilinear motion—the same rationale as our magnitude-weighted phase loss. PAE P /PAE S use fixed 2 s windows anchored at the TauP-predicted arrivals. Two random directions yield an expected PAE of 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} . To support the “near chance” characterization, we compute 95% bootstrap confidence intervals for PAE S (10,000 resamples over the 861 test events): GAN [ 56.7 , 61.4 ] [56.7,61.4] and HEGGS [ 52.7 , 57.3 ] [52.7,57.3] both include the 57.3 ∘ 57.3^{\\circ} random-direction expectation, i.e., their S-wave polarization is statistically indistinguishable from chance; RIPPLE (GL) [ 49.5 , 54.2 ] [49.5,54.2] sits slightly but significantly below it; RIPPLE [ 30.8 , 36.9 ] [30.8,36.9] is separated from chance by over 20 ∘ 20^{\\circ} . Appendix C Ablation Results Tables 11 and 12 extend the two domains of Table 4 to all metrics. The behavior of the omitted metrics supports the column selection in the main text. Table 11: Full ablation on Spatial LibriSpeech (cross-room), extending the spatial columns of Table 4 . Each row removes one component from RIPPLE while keeping the magnitude path and all other settings fixed. w/o source-coherent init replaces ϕ init = ∠ ​ X ( s ) \\phi_{\\text{init}}=\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})} with random phase initialization while keeping the Griffin–Lim prior; w/o GL prior replaces p 0 p_{0} with a Gaussian prior; w/o IPD sets λ 4 = 0 \\lambda_{4}{=}0 ; coupled timesteps ties t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} . Lower is better. Fidelity Spatial accuracy Configuration Wave L2 ↓ \\downarrow Amp. L2 ↓ \\downarrow Phase L2 ↓ \\downarrow MRSTFT ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} ↓ \\downarrow L ​ 1 φ L1_{\\varphi} ↓ \\downarrow Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} ↓ \\downarrow RIPPLE 0.019 0.432 0.619 1.249 0.243 0.152 0.319 w/o source-coherent init 0.018 0.448 0.683 1.234 0.253 0.161 0.332 w/o Griffin–Lim prior 0.019 0.581 1.404 1.413 1.616 0.615 1.604 w/o IPD loss ( λ 4 = 0 \\lambda_{4}{=}0 ) 0.022 0.502 1.451 1.283 1.627 0.188 1.625 coupled timesteps ( t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} ) 0.021 0.557 0.807 1.432 1.582 0.200 1.581 RIPPLE (GL) 0.022 0.460 1.072 1.318 1.591 0.395 1.586 Spatial (Table 11 ). Waveform-level fidelity is essentially insensitive to the phase-side configuration: Wave L2 stays within 0.018 0.018 – 0.022 0.022 across all rows, showing that with the magnitude path fixed, the time-domain envelope is preserved even under phase collapse. In contrast, Phase L2 and the angular metrics separate by up to a fivefold regression across configurations; this contrast itself reconfirms, at the metric level, the premise that spatial information is carried by phase alignment rather than amplitude. The coupled-timestep row shows the separation most sharply: Phase L2 degrades mildly ( 0.807 0.807 ) while L ​ 1 θ L1_{\\theta} collapses ( 1.582 1.582 ), localizing the damage of timestep coupling to inter-channel alignment rather than per-bin phase accuracy. Table 12: Full ablation on SCEDC (cross-station), extending the seismic columns of Table 4 . GOF scores in [ 0 , 100 ] [0,100] , higher is better; particle-motion errors, lower is better (random-direction baseline: PAE = 57.3 ∘ =57.3^{\\circ} ). Intensity-measure definitions in Appendix B.4 . All configurations receive identical conditioning, including the source-station waveform. Goodness-of-fit (GOF) ↑ \\uparrow Inter-component particle motion ↓ \\downarrow Configuration PGA PSA FAS Env. I A I_{A} D S D_{S} Mean PAE RLE PAE P RLE P PAE S RLE S RIPPLE 90.56 55.86 47.01 85.75 67.73 65.85 68.79 32.57 0.111 26.48 0.121 33.81 0.098 w/o source-coherent init 87.15 52.49 47.13 85.41 55.31 63.33 65.13 43.28 0.142 33.17 0.169 48.23 0.110 w/o Griffin–Lim prior 67.03 52.58 47.45 85.05 39.26 64.97 59.39 52.07 0.167 36.54 0.187 57.33 0.170 w/o IPD loss ( λ 4 = 0 \\lambda_{4}{=}0 ) 70.38 53.24 46.92 84.49 41.10 64.11 60.04 52.38 0.171 35.42 0.183 57.31 0.170 coupled timesteps ( t m = t p t_{m}{=}t_{p} ) 72.98 50.99 45.31 85.12 38.44 54.2 57.84 50.98 0.135 34.33 0.170 50.28 0.115 RIPPLE (GL) 81.96 53.05 47.61 85.61 41.20 65.64 62.51 49.79 0.169 40.42 0.181 51.87 0.153 Seismic (Table 12 ). The six GOF measures split into two groups by their sensitivity to phase learning. FAS ( 46.9 46.9 – 47.6 47.6 ), envelope correlation ( 84.5 84.5 – 85.8 85.8 ), and significant duration ( 63.3 63.3 – 65.9 65.9 ) are essentially invariant across all configurations—purely spectral or low-frequency envelope-level quantities determined by the magnitude path alone; this is the full evidence behind the main-text observation that purely spectral quantities are unchanged. In contrast, PGA ( 67.0 67.0 – 90.6 90.6 ) and Arias intensity ( 39.3 39.3 – 67.7 67.7 ), both sensitive to time-domain reassembly, separate widely. The same split appears in the learned-phase gains over the Griffin–Lim ablation: the improvement concentrates on time-domain intensity measures sensitive to waveform reassembly ( I A I_{A} 41.2 → 67.7 41.2\\to 67.7 , PGA 82.0 → 90.6 82.0\\to 90.6 ), while purely spectral quantities are unchanged (FAS 47.6 47.6 vs. 47.0 47.0 ). Notably, under w/o source-coherent init , PGA drops only mildly ( 87.2 87.2 ) while I A I_{A} regresses substantially ( 67.7 → 55.3 67.7\\to 55.3 ): accumulating energy over the full waveform ( I A I_{A} ) demands sustained phase alignment in a way that an instantaneous peak (PGA) does not. The RLE family moves in the same direction as PAE throughout—RLE S converges to an identical 0.170 0.170 under both w/o GL and w/o IPD —confirming that polarization direction and rectilinearity are two views of the same cross-component structure, which is why the main text reports PAE only. C.1 Sensitivity to prior iterations K K . Figure 5: Sensitivity of Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} to the number of Griffin–Lim prior iterations K K at inference (same 100 test pairs as Figure 4, integrated with N = 50 N{=}50 Heun steps). The flow is trained with K = 8 K{=}8 (dashed line). Lower is better. The number of Griffin–Lim iterations K K controls the quality of the prior: larger K K improves magnitude consistency but, since the iteration is per-channel, gradually attenuates the source’s inter-channel structure. Since retraining per K K is beyond our compute budget, we train with a fixed K = 8 K{=}8 and vary K K at inference only; the resulting curve (Figure 5 ) therefore measures sensitivity to prior quality under a fixed flow, conflating prior degradation with train–test prior mismatch. Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} stays within a narrow band ( 0.315 0.315 – 0.376 0.376 for K ∈ [ 0 , 16 ] K\\in[0,16] )—a spread an order of magnitude smaller than the gap to the Gaussian-prior level ( ≈ 1.5 \\approx\\!1.5 ; Figure 4 )—and even K = 0 K{=}0 , the source phase with no magnitude projection, remains far below that level. The trained flow is thus insensitive to the exact prior quality it is integrated from: for this model, the choice of prior (Figure 4 and Table 4 ) is first-order, while inference-time K K is not a sensitive hyperparameter; whether the same holds for flows trained at other K K remains untested. The K = 0 K=0 endpoint deserves separate comment. There the prior is the source phase with no magnitude projection applied: a valid point of 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} carrying the source’s inter-channel structure, but not magnitude-consistent. This is the projection-free prior identified in the Discussion as separating the two properties that the Gaussian ablation removes jointly. Its Δ Spatial-Angle \\Delta_{\\text{Spatial-Angle}} of 0.376 sits far closer to the full model (0.319) than to the Gaussian prior (1.604), indicating that valid 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} structure together with source coherence—rather than magnitude consistency—accounts for most of the prior’s contribution. The evidence is partial: K K is varied at inference under a flow trained with K = 8 K=8 , so prior degradation is confounded with train–test mismatch. Appendix D Training and Inference Algorithms D.1 Training and Inference Details Algorithms 1 and 2 give pseudocode for the training step and the two-stage inference procedure. Here we expand the integration scheme summarized in the main text. Magnitude stage. We integrate the magnitude flow from log ⁡ | X ( s ) | \\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}| to a predicted target magnitude log ⁡ | X ^ ( t ) | \\log|\\hat{X}^{(\\mathrm{t})}| with a Heun second-order scheme along the uniform timestep grid { t i } i = 0 N \\{t_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{N} ( t i = i / N t_{i}=i/N ), and set m ^ = exp ⁡ ( z ) \\hat{m}=\\exp(z) . Prior construction. From the predicted target magnitude m ^ \\hat{m} and the source phase ∠ ​ X ( s ) \\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})} we construct the source-informed Griffin–Lim prior p 0 p_{0} (Eq. 3 ). This mirrors the training-time prior, except that training uses the oracle target magnitude | X ( t ) | |X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| while inference uses the predicted m ^ \\hat{m} . Phase stage. We integrate the phase ODE from p 0 p_{0} using the same Heun scheme. At every phase step, the predicted log-magnitude z z is concatenated along the channel dimension as conditioning, matching the training input format. After integration we project the phase prediction onto 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} by unit normalization, reassemble the complex STFT X ^ = m ^ ⊙ ( cos ^ + i ​ sin ^ ) \\hat{X}=\\hat{m}\\odot(\\hat{\\cos}+i\\,\\hat{\\sin}) , and recover the waveform by inverse STFT. We use N = 50 N=50 as a default. D.2 Geometry of the phase flow The phase flow is defined on 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} but integrated in ℝ 2 \\mathbb{R}^{2} . The interpolant of Eq. 6 traces the chord between p 0 p_{0} and ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) (\\cos\\angle X^{(t)},\\sin\\angle X^{(t)}) , so for t p ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) t_{p}\\in(0,1) the training state lies strictly inside the unit circle: the parameterization is ambient by construction, not only at its endpoints. This makes the perturbation σ prior ​ ϵ \\sigma_{\\text{prior}}\\epsilon in Eq. 3 structural rather than incidental. With σ prior = 0 \\sigma_{\\text{prior}}=0 , training states would concentrate on the thin family of chords joining prior to target, leaving the velocity field unconstrained in the radial direction—while integration departs from that family immediately, since the Heun predictor is a straight extrapolation in ℝ 2 \\mathbb{R}^{2} and the corrector averages two velocities evaluated at different radii. The network would then be queried away from the states it was trained on. The perturbation thickens the support into a neighborhood of the chords, and the two mechanisms that return predictions to the circle act at separate points: the unit-norm penalty in ℒ phase q \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{phase}}^{q} pulls the one-step prediction toward 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} during training, and the final unit normalization (Appendix D.1 ) enforces it exactly at inference. The scale is bounded above by what it is meant to leave intact: σ prior \\sigma_{\\text{prior}} must remain small relative to the inter-channel structure carried by p 0 p_{0} , since a large perturbation destroys precisely the coherence that source-informed initialization supplies. We fix σ prior = 0.1 \\sigma_{\\text{prior}}=0.1 throughout and do not tune it; sensitivity to this choice is untested, note also that σ prior \\sigma_{\\text{prior}} varies prior quality along a different axis than the K K sweep of Appendix C.1 . Why not a manifold-native flow. Riemannian flow matching defines interpolant and velocity on the manifold and would remove the ambient excursion above. We keep the embedded ℝ 2 \\mathbb{R}^{2} parameterization for two reasons. First, it leaves the phase head architecturally identical to the magnitude head, so the two velocity networks differ only in input channels and share the conditioning encoder E c E_{c} without map-specific machinery. Second, the predicted log-magnitude is concatenated to the phase head’s input at every integration step; in the ambient parameterization this is a plain channel concatenation, whereas a manifold-native flow would place state and conditioning in different spaces. What the ambient view loses is restored where it matters: the IPD loss (Eq 11 ) normalizes predictions onto 𝕊 1 \\mathbb{S}^{1} before forming relative phase vectors, so the coherence objective is evaluated on the manifold regardless of where the flow is integrated. Algorithm 1 Training step 0: paired sample ( w ( s ) , w ( t ) ) (w^{(\\mathrm{s})},w^{(\\mathrm{t})}) , conditioning c c , loss weights λ 1 , … , λ 5 \\lambda_{1},\\dots,\\lambda_{5} 1: X ( s ) ← ℱ ​ { w ( s ) } X^{(s)}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{F}\\{w^{(\\mathrm{s})}\\} , X ( t ) ← ℱ ​ { w ( t ) } X^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{F}\\{w^{(\\mathrm{t})}\\} 2: // Source-informed phase prior 3: ϕ GL ← GL K ​ ( | X ( t ) | ; ∠ ​ X ( s ) ) \\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}}\\leftarrow\\mathrm{GL}_{K}\\!\\big(|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|;\\,\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{s})}\\big) 4: p 0 ← ( cos ⁡ ϕ GL , sin ⁡ ϕ GL ) + σ prior ​ ϵ p_{0}\\leftarrow(\\cos\\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}},\\sin\\phi_{\\mathrm{GL}})+\\sigma_{\\text{prior}}\\epsilon , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I) 5: // Independent timesteps 6: Sample t m , t p ∼ 𝒰 ​ ( 0 , 1 ) t_{m},t_{p}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0,1) independently 7: // Interpolation and target velocities 8: z t m mag ← ( 1 − t m ) ​ log ⁡ | X ( s ) | + t m ​ log ⁡ | X ( t ) | z^{\\text{mag}}_{t_{m}}\\leftarrow(1{-}t_{m})\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}|+t_{m}\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}| 9: z t p phase ← ( 1 − t p ) ​ p 0 + t p ​ ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) z^{\\text{phase}}_{t_{p}}\\leftarrow(1{-}t_{p})\\,p_{0}+t_{p}\\,(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})}) 10: v mag ∗ ← log ⁡ | X ( t ) | − log ⁡ | X ( s ) | v^{*}_{\\text{mag}}\\leftarrow\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|-\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{s})}| 11: v phase ∗ ← ( cos ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) , sin ⁡ ∠ ​ X ( t ) ) − p 0 v^{*}_{\\text{phase}}\\leftarrow(\\cos\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})},\\sin\\angle X^{(\\mathrm{t})})-p_{0} 12: // Velocity predictions 13: h ← E c ​ ( c ) h\\leftarrow E_{c}(c) 14: v θ mag ← v θ mag ​ ( z t m mag , t m , h ) v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta}\\leftarrow v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta}\\!\\big(z^{\\text{mag}}_{t_{m}},\\,t_{m},\\,h\\big) 15: v θ phase ← v θ phase ​ ( [ z t p phase , log ⁡ | X ( t ) | ] , t p , h ) v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta}\\leftarrow v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta}\\!\\big([z^{\\text{phase}}_{t_{p}},\\,\\log|X^{(\\mathrm{t})}|],\\,t_{p},\\,h\\big) {phase head concats target magnitude} 16: // One-step predictions for auxiliary losses 17: z ^ 1 | t m mag ← z t m mag + ( 1 − t m ) ​ v θ mag \\hat{z}^{\\text{mag}}_{1|t_{m}}\\leftarrow z^{\\text{mag}}_{t_{m}}+(1{-}t_{m})\\,v^{\\text{mag}}_{\\theta} 18: z ^ 1 | t p phase ← z t p phase + ( 1 − t p ) ​ v θ phase \\hat{z}^{\\text{phase}}_{1|t_{p}}\\leftarrow z^{\\text{phase}}_{t_{p}}+(1{-}t_{p})\\,v^{\\text{phase}}_{\\theta} 19: // Compose loss (Eq. 12 ) 20: ℒ ← λ 1 ​ ℒ mag + λ 2 ​ ℒ phase vel + λ 3 ​ ℒ phase q + λ 4 ​ ℒ IPD + λ 5 ​ ℒ rec \\mathcal{L}\\leftarrow\\lambda_{1}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mag}}+\\lambda_{2}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{vel}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{3}\\mathcal{L}^{\\text{q}}_{\\text{phase}}+\\lambda_{4}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IPD}}+\\lambda_{5}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rec}} 21: Update θ \\\n", "numLines": 2, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2}}}
